// Degenerate.schema — the degenerate ARRANGEMENTS (issue #203).
//
// Every other file here carries realistic protocol structure, and that is
// exactly what made the corpus blind: a real shape always has enough
// neighboring fields to mask the arrangements an emitter's run/chunk
// machinery keys on. A fixed scalar array that is essentially the whole
// message; a unit whose every type is two floats and nothing else; a nested
// struct that lands across a run boundary. The Go flat word codec wrote
// `values [2]float64` TWICE — 32 bytes where every other language wrote 16 —
// and every Go-to-Go round trip stayed green, because nothing in the corpus
// had that shape.
//
// So the types here are deliberately unrealistic, and that is their whole
// job. They are held to the same C++-pinned wire goldens as the rest of the
// corpus, in all nine languages, on every push.
//
// TWO STANDING PROPERTIES OF THIS FILE. First, every type's wire width is a
// whole number of BYTES. The legs whose emitters write a whole message into
// a buffer (Dart, Java, Elixir) cannot append to a bit stream, so they
// concatenate their per-message buffers; that equals what the stream legs
// write only while no shape ends mid-byte.
//
// Second, no type here may pull a target's float-bits helpers in. The
// bare-float-unit defect — an unused `math` import in generated Go — can
// only manifest in a file with no other math consumer, so a type whose
// floats reach those helpers belongs elsewhere. The generated-source goldens
// pin the import block, so a change is loud.

package example

// ---- a bare float unit --------------------------------------------------

// Two floats and nothing else: the Vec2/Vec3/Quat shape a project declares
// before it declares anything else. Types.schema's own Vec3 survives only
// because that file has other float users.
type Vec2
{
    x float64
    y float64
}

// ---- fixed scalar arrays ------------------------------------------------

// A fixed scalar array as essentially the whole message — the arrangement
// that wrote its elements twice.
type SpanF64
{
    values [2]float64
}

// The same arrangement over the integer storages.
type SpanU64
{
    values [2]uint64
}

type SpanI64
{
    values [2]int64
}

// [1]T: the one-element neighbor, where a loop and an unrolled element
// differ by nothing but the emitter's decision to take either.
type SpanOne
{
    values [1]uint64
}

// [N]T landing exactly on a 64-bit chunk boundary.
type SpanChunk
{
    values [4]uint16
}

// An array with a trailing field: the elements and the field are
// consecutive statically-sized items and share whatever run holds them.
type SpanTail
{
    values [2]float64
    tail   uint32
}

// Two such arrays back to back.
type SpanTwice
{
    a [2]float64
    b [2]float64
}

// ---- nested structs at the edges ----------------------------------------

// Narrow on purpose: three fields under one word is the shape a parent wants
// to place itself rather than call for. The widths sum to a whole 64-bit
// word for the reason stated at the top of this file: every type here is a
// byte multiple, so the nine legs agree on one golden whether their emitter
// writes into a shared stream or a buffer per message.
type Trio
{
    a bits(20)
    b bits(20)
    c bits(24)
}

// A nested struct as the ONLY field.
type TrioSole
{
    inner Trio
}

// A nested struct as the FIRST field.
type TrioFirst
{
    inner   Trio
    trailer bits(16)
}

// A nested struct STRADDLING a run boundary: the prefix is sized so Trio's
// fields land on either side of the Go emitter's 384-bit run cap (flat.go,
// maxRunBits) — the arrangement that made that emitter name a nested field
// on the OUTER type. The prefix width is calibrated to that cap, so
// internal/codegen/golang's own test states the property against the
// constant; TrioSole and TrioFirst carry the cap-independent halves.
type TrioStraddle
{
    pad0  bits(64)
    pad1  bits(64)
    pad2  bits(64)
    pad3  bits(64)
    pad4  bits(64)
    pad5  bits(24)
    inner Trio
}
